---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import FurtherReading from '../../components/FurtherReading.astro';
import SeaBreak from '../../components/SeaBreak.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
import Layers from '../../components/Layers.astro';
import { celestial, ocean } from '../../config/images';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'The Angles', href: '/explore/angles/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Angles: Ascendant, Descendant, Midheaven, and IC"
  description="The Ascendant, Descendant, Midheaven, and IC form the chart's four angles. Learn what each marks, why birth time matters, and how they organize the chart."
  path="/explore/angles/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
        <h1>The four angles</h1>
        <p class="lede">The Ascendant, Descendant, Midheaven, and IC form a structural cross through the chart. They connect the zodiac to a particular place and moment on Earth, which is why birth time matters so much.</p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img"
        src={ocean.cliffsSeaFog.src}
        srcset={ocean.cliffsSeaFog.srcset}
        sizes="100vw"
        alt={ocean.cliffsSeaFog.alt}
        width={ocean.cliffsSeaFog.width}
        height={ocean.cliffsSeaFog.height}
        style="transform: scaleX(-1);"
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <Layers kind="astronomy">
        <p>
          The angles are calculated from the relationship between the ecliptic, the local horizon,
          and the local meridian at a specific place and time. Because the Earth rotates, these
          points move through the zodiac over the course of a day. Even a modest difference in birth
          time can shift their degrees, and a larger difference can change the Ascendant entirely.
          Whether a small time error matters in practice depends on the chart and the technique being
          used. <a href="/birth-time/">The birth-time guide</a> explains where precision matters most.
        </p>
      </Layers>

      <h2>The Ascendant (ASC)</h2>
      <p>
        The Ascendant is the zodiac degree rising over the eastern horizon at birth. It is the point
        from which the chart's houses are organized and is often associated with the way a person
        enters experience, approaches new situations, and presents themselves instinctively. It is
        what people usually mean by the <a href="/explore/the-big-three/">rising sign</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>The Descendant (DSC)</h2>
      <p>
        The Descendant lies exactly opposite the Ascendant on the western horizon. It is associated
        with one-to-one relationships and the kinds of qualities, dynamics, and encounters we meet
        through other people. Modern psychological traditions may also discuss projection here, but
        that is one interpretive framework rather than the only way to read the point.
      </p>

      <h2>The Midheaven (MC)</h2>
      <p>
        The Midheaven, from the Latin <em>Medium Coeli</em>, marks the upper meridian of the chart.
        It is strongly associated with public life, direction, reputation, vocation, and what becomes
        visible about us in the world. In some house systems it defines the 10th-house cusp; in others
        it may fall elsewhere while retaining its own significance.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>

  <SeaBreak image={celestial.threshold} />

  <section class="section section--sea">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>The Imum Coeli (IC)</h2>
      <p>
        The IC, or <em>Imum Coeli</em>, lies opposite the Midheaven at the lower meridian. It is
        associated with home, roots, family history, private foundations, and the part of life that
        supports what becomes visible elsewhere. As with every angle, its meaning deepens through the
        sign, ruler, nearby planets, and the rest of the chart.
      </p>

      <h2>Why angularity carries weight</h2>
      <p>
        The angles are not planets; they are calculated points. But planets close to them are often
        given extra emphasis because they are positioned at the chart's most prominent axes. Older
        Western traditions formalize this under <a href="/explore/dignities/">accidental dignity</a>.
        I find angularity useful because it tells me where a planet may be especially visible or
        active, but I still want to know what that planet is, how it is conditioned, and what else it
        is connected to.
      </p>
      <p>
        Together, the ASC/DSC and MC/IC axes organize several fundamental polarities: self and other,
        private and public, emergence and foundation. They provide structure, not a script.
      </p>

      <Layers kind="differs">
        <p>
          The Ascendant and Midheaven are astronomical points, but astrologers differ in how they
          derive the intermediate house cusps from them. In whole sign houses, for example, the MC
          does not necessarily define the 10th-house cusp. This is one reason two astrologers can use
          the same birth data and still display somewhat different house layouts.
        </p>
      </Layers>

      <div class="callout">
        <p style="margin: 0;">
          <strong>Don't know your birth time?</strong> The angles and houses are the parts most
          affected by that uncertainty. Many planetary positions remain usable.
          <a href="/birth-time/">See where to look for the time and what remains usable if you cannot find it.</a>
        </p>
      </div>
      <FurtherReading />
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="Want to understand why birth time changes the chart?"
    body="The angles are where time and place enter the chart most visibly. The birth-time guide shows what depends on them, what does not, and how to handle uncertainty."
    buttonLabel="Read the Birth Time Guide"
    buttonHref="/birth-time/"
    secondaryLabel="Keep exploring chart structure"
    secondaryHref="/explore/birth-chart-basics/"
  />
</BaseLayout>
